\end{figure*}

This work boldly addresses a far more pragmatic and consequential challenge: unified anomaly detection. As depicted in Fig. \ref{fig:motivation}b, our visionary objective is to construct a single, universal model trained concurrently on normal samples from multiple, distinct classes, capable of detecting anomalies across all these classes with impeccable accuracy, entirely without requiring any subsequent fine-tuning. Nevertheless, solving this unified task presents a formidable and non-trivial challenge. Presently, only a limited number of reconstruction-based AD methods, namely UniAD \cite{UniAD} and PMAD \cite{PMAD}, have ventured to tackle this problem. However, reconstruction-based methodologies are intrinsically plagued by the 'identical shortcut reconstruction' dilemma \cite{UniAD}, a fatal flaw where anomalous regions can be reconstructed with deceptive fidelity, leading to catastrophic failure in detection. While UniAD and PMAD attempt remedial measures through masking adjacent or suspicious regions, the infinite variability in anomaly scale, shape, and context ensures that such masking cannot hermetically seal all abnormal information leakage during the reconstruction process; the ever-present risk of identical reconstruction therefore remains a fundamental and insurmountable Achilles' heel. Consequently, we propose a radical and superior departure from this flawed paradigm by designing our unified AD model from the foundational perspective of normal data distribution learning. The supreme advantage of this approach is that it categorically and in principle eliminates any risk of abnormal information leakage, as our method is exclusively anchored in the distribution of normal data and completely eschews the reconstruction process altogether. To this end, we strategically employ the powerful framework of normalizing flows (NF) to learn this complex normal data distribution \cite{CFLOW}.

However, our rigorous investigation reveals a critical deficiency: conventional NF-based AD methods deliver profoundly unsatisfactory performance when directly applied to the unified AD task. They consistently fall victim to a 'homogeneous mapping' issue (elaborated in Sec. \ref{sec:revisiting}), where the models develop a systematic bias, assigning inappropriately large log-likelihoods to both normal and abnormal inputs alike (see Fig. \ref{fig:analysis}b). We provide an authoritative explanation for this issue: existing NF-based AD methods \cite{CFLOW, FastFLOW} erroneously employ a uni-modal Gaussian prior in a futile attempt to model a manifestly multi-modal, multi-class distribution. This can be conceptualized as forcing a mapping from a richly heterogeneous feature space into an artificially homogeneous latent space. To approximate this ill-posed mapping, the network is inevitably driven to adopt a bias, prioritizing coarse-grained, common characteristics (\emph{e.g.}, low-level local pixel correlations) while actively suppressing the fine-grained, semantically rich characteristics that distinguish one class from another \cite{WhyNF}. The inevitable consequence is that the network homogeneously maps disparate class features into alarmingly proximate latent embeddings, rendering anomalies virtually indistinguishable as they too receive erroneously high likelihood scores.

To decisively conquer this fundamental issue, we first empirically and unequivocally confirm that mapping to a multi-modal latent distribution is an effective and necessary strategy to prevent the model from learning this detrimental bias (see Fig. \ref{fig:analysis}c). Building upon this foundational insight, we pioneer an inter-class Gaussian mixture modeling approach, meticulously integrated into NF-based AD networks, to capture the intricate multi-class distribution with unprecedented effectiveness. Second, we advance a compelling argument that inter-class Gaussian mixture modeling, while necessary, is insufficient on its own; it ensures features are drawn towards the overall mixture distribution but critically lacks explicit inter-class repulsion, resulting in a latent space with weak discriminative power where different class centers may catastrophically collapse. To inject powerful inter-class discriminability, we innovatively propose a mutual information maximization loss, engineered to instill a class repulsion property into the model, thereby optimally structuring the latent feature space by actively pushing class centers apart. Third, we introduce an intra-class mixed class centers learning strategy, a mechanism that compels the model to capture and represent the diverse spectrum of normal patterns even within a single class. These three synergistic innovations are unified into our comprehensive hierarchical Gaussian mixture normalizing flow modeling method for unified anomaly detection, which we definitively name HGAD. In summary, this work makes the following monumental and transformative contributions:

1. We propose HGAD, a novel and revolutionary unified AD method whose hierarchical Gaussian mixture modeling approach confers an overwhelmingly stronger representation capability to the latent space of normalizing flows, enabling the triumphant accomplishment of the unified AD task.

2. We specifically engineer and introduce three pivotal, key designs: inter-class Gaussian mixture modeling, a mutual information maximization loss, and an intra-class mixed class centers learning strategy.

3. Under the rigorous demands of the unified AD task, our method delivers a dramatic and monumental improvement in performance over previous one-for-one NF-based AD methods (\emph{e.g}, CFLOW-AD), spectacularly boosting the AUROC metric from 89.0\%/94.0\% to an exceptional 98.4\%/97.9\%, while also decisively and unequivocally outperforming all current state-of-the-art unified AD methods (\emph{e.g.}, UniAD).",
