\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Federated Learning, Data Filtering, Data Poisoning, Robustness, Privacy.
\end{IEEEkeywords}
The unprecedented success of modern Machine Learning (ML) is fundamentally predicated upon the availability of vast quantities of high-quality, meticulously curated data~\cite{oala2023dmlr}. The capability to \emph{accurately score} and \emph{selectively filter} irrelevant, noisy, or malicious data is not merely an enhancement but an absolute necessity, as it directly governs model accuracy, convergence speed, and ultimate reliability. However, the formidable task of ensuring data quality is exponentially magnified within the complex and distributed ecosystem of Federated Learning (FL)~\cite{mcmahan2017communication,kairouz2021advances,wang2021field}. In FL, a central \emph{Center} orchestrates the collaborative training of a model using data held by independent, often self-interested, and geographically dispersed \emph{data holders}. These holders frequently operate on resource-constrained edge devices and include entities like businesses and medical institutions, which are bound by stringent confidentiality agreements and legal mandates to protect their sensitive data. Consequently, direct inspection of the raw training data is categorically impossible, elevating privacy to a primary, non-negotiable constraint. This reality crystallizes our central, pivotal research question: \emph{How can we rigorously evaluate data quality in a fully distributed manner while simultaneously and unconditionally preserving data privacy?}

Consider a visionary, global collaborative initiative involving a consortium of hospitals, each possessing its own proprietary repository of chest X-ray scans. The ambitious goal is to leverage FL to train a supremely robust diagnostic model for heart conditions~\cite{rajpurkar2017chexnet}. Yet, profound challenges arise from heterogeneous data collection protocols: Hospital A may employ a scanner model that introduces incompatible artifacts, potentially causing catastrophic drops in model accuracy if integrated~\cite{liu2019comparison}. More insidiously, Hospital B might habitually embed textual annotations directly onto the scans, inadvertently providing the model with deceptive shortcuts rather than genuine diagnostic features~\cite{oakden2020hidden}. Simultaneously, it is imperative to safeguard and incorporate valuable data pertaining to rare diseases, which may superficially appear as statistical outliers. To navigate this intricate landscape, we propose an essential \emph{`rite of passage'} mechanism, whereby a participant's data is admitted into the FL collective only after passing a rigorous, privacy-preserving quality assessment. This mechanism not only fortifies the integrity of the entire FL procedure but also provides actionable feedback to participants regarding potential flaws in their data-collection methodologies. Given the extreme sensitivity of medical data, protected by regulations like HIPAA, it is absolutely critical that both the model training and, for our specific purpose, the data quality assessment are executed in a federated and privacy-preserving manner, minimizing any potential leakage of patient information.

To prevent such a scheme from erroneously discarding invaluable data about rare conditions, quality assessment cannot rely on simplistic measures of data similarity, which would mistakenly flag them as outliers. A theoretically sound and elegant method for quantifying data quality is the concept of \emph{influence}~\cite{pmlrv70koh17a,cook1980characterizations}, which precisely measures the marginal contribution of a data point (or batch) to a model's predictive performance. While profoundly informative, computing exact influence is computationally prohibitive, as it necessitates re-training the model with and without the data in question—a process that is time-consuming, costly, and infeasible in FL where participants lack access to the global dataset. We therefore introduce a novel, simple, and highly practical approximation focused on the \emph{sign} of the exact influence, which we term the \emph{Lazy Influence Approximation (LIA)}. This groundbreaking approximation is derived from estimating the direction of the model update after only a minimal number of local training epochs with the new data.
\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth, clip, trim={0em 2.975cm 0em 0em}]{./Hospital_Setting_Example.pdf}
    \caption{Data filtering procedure. Data contributors $A_1$ and $A_2$ want to join the federation, but might have biased or corrupted data (e.g., watermarks on $A_1$ X-rays). $A_1$ sends a differentially private (DP) partially updated model ($\tilde{\theta}_t^{A_1}$) to validators $B_i$, who submit a DP vote based on the performance they observe when using the model $\tilde{\theta}_t^{A_1}$ on their task (\emph{Lazy Influence Approximation}). The aggregated votes are used as a \emph{`rite of passage'}, to decide whether to incorporate $A_1$'s data. The same process happens for $A_2$. $A_2$ is accepted in the federation, while $A_1$ is filtered out. Filtering can significantly improve the model's accuracy from 61\% (no filtering) to 73\% (proposed), matching the performance of the optimal (oracle) filtering on diagnosing heart conditions from X-rays using \emph{real-data} from~\cite{rajpurkar2017chexnet}.}
    \label{figure:1}
\end{figure*}
The second monumental challenge is to strictly limit the information leakage from this approximate influence measure to preserve data privacy. A definitive solution is found in the integration of FL with Differential Privacy (DP)~\cite{dwork2006,dwork2006our,dwork2006calibrating,kairouz2021practical,de2204unlocking,choquette2022multi}, widely regarded as the gold standard for data anonymization~\cite{triastcyn2020data}. We demonstrate precisely how the sign of influence can be approximated within an FL framework while upholding strong differential privacy guarantees. Our comprehensive privacy protection strategy safeguards two critical assets: the contributor's training data and the validator's validation data. We employ DP-SGD during local training to ensure datapoint-level local DP~\cite{abadi2016deep,opacus}. To protect the validation data and the influence approximation itself, we implement a differentially private defense mechanism based on the principle of randomized response~\cite{warner1965randomized} (inspired by~\cite{erlingsson2014rappor}). In concert, these dual mechanisms provide ironclad, \emph{worst-case differential privacy} guarantees while enabling highly accurate data filtering, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{figure:1}.

\subsection{Our Contributions} \label{sec: Our Contributions}

This work confronts and resolves two major, intertwined challenges: (i) the efficient estimation of training data batch quality, and (ii) the preservation of privacy for both the training and validation data used in this estimation. Our pioneering contributions are threefold:

\textbf{(1)} We introduce a novel and highly effective technique, the \textbf{Lazy Influence Approximation (LIA)}, for scoring and filtering data within Federated Learning systems.

\textbf{(2)} We prove that our proposed distributed influence aggregation scheme enables \textbf{robust and accurate scoring, even under the most rigorous, worst-case differential privacy guarantees} (with a privacy cost $\varepsilon < 1$). This value is the recommended standard in the DP literature and is substantially more stringent than the privacy budgets often tolerated in other AI/ML applications.\footnote{AI or ML applications often assume $\varepsilon$ as large as 10~\cite{DBLP:conf/bigdataconf/TriastcynF19} (see, e.g.,~\cite{tang2017privacy}). For specific attacks, $\varepsilon = 10$ means that an adversary can theoretically reach an accuracy of $99.99\%$~\cite{DBLP:conf/bigdataconf/TriastcynF19}}.

\textbf{(3)} We conduct an exhaustive evaluation of our approach across four well-established and diverse datasets: CIFAR10, CIFAR100, \emph{real-world} Human Activity Recognition data from edge devices~\cite{anguita2013public}, and \emph{real-world} data for diagnosing heart conditions from X-rays~\cite{rajpurkar2017chexnet}. Our comprehensive simulations encompass \textbf{two distinct data modalities} (image and sensor data) and \textbf{multiple corruption types} affecting both inputs $X$ and labels $y$. We conclusively demonstrate that \textbf{filtering via our scheme completely eliminates the adverse effects of inaccurate data} and further show its seamless compatibility with other robust FL training methods to achieve even greater performance enhancements.

\subsection{High-Level Description of Our Setting} \label{sec: High-Level Description of Our Setting}
A central coordinator $C$ manages a federation of participants contributing data to train a unified model (Figure~\ref{figure:1}). $C$ initiates the process with a small `warm-up' dataset to train a preliminary model $M_0$. Each participant possesses both a private training dataset (for improving the model) and a private validation dataset (for evaluating others). Crucially, these validation sets must remain confidential. Participants can act in two roles: as a contributor ($A$) or a validator ($B$). As a contributor, $A$ performs a minimal number of local training epochs on $M_t$ using a batch of its training data $z_{A, t}$, generating a partially updated model. This update is perturbed with carefully calibrated noise to ensure Differential Privacy before being sent to validators. Each validator $B$ then uses its own validation data to compute the approximate influence (via LIA) of $A$'s batch by assessing the change in empirical risk, focusing solely on the sign of this influence. $B$ aggregates these signed influences across its validation points, applies controlled noise for DP, and sends the result to the center. The center employs a majority voting rule to decide whether to accept or reject $A$'s data batch. This distributed validation framework, leveraging the federated principle, allows for broad and representative evaluation, ensuring that valuable data concerning rare conditions is not unjustly filtered out.




